 "panel_e": [
  {
   "pair": "information_seeking_under_uncertainty + switching_cost",
   "role": "synergistic",
   "sd": 0.001924500897298701,
   "sign3": true,
   "heading": "Synergistic example  –  information-seeking under uncertainty + switching cost",
   "observations": "Observations. The pair overshoots the additive expectation in Cognitive flexibility & adaptation (additive 0.29 → real 0.48) and Repetitive/restricted behavior (additive 0.30 → real 0.46), and it reaches Δ 0.30 in Novelty/uncertainty & sensory behavior – a dimension neither single factor affects at all (a new phenotype). All 3 replicates agree at the 6-dimension aggregate level (SD 0.002); the error bars give the per-dimension replicate SD.",
   "possible_cause": "Possible cause of synergy. The two lesions sit at the two ends of one loop: information seeking is what turns uncertainty into a probe (ask, explore, test), while switching cost is what prices leaving the current task set. Either lesion alone leaves the loop runnable – a system that stops probing can still switch when pushed, and a system that switches cheaply can still act on a probe it generates. Losing both closes the loop: nothing probes and nothing releases the current set, so the same single-factor damage compounds instead of adding up, and it reaches a dimension neither lesion touched.",
   "text_on_figure": false,
